            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering using only non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x=i\\;\\big|\\;i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non‑sunny.  \nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-2\\)*, the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n*If \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(b\\le1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus each element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with **zero** sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nChoose two distinct integers \\(b_1,b_2\\) with \\(1\\le b_1<b_2\\le n+1-i\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y-(b_1+1)=m\\,(x-i),\\qquad \nm\\neq0,\\;m\\neq-1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\) is any real number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) (for instance \\(m=1\\)).  \nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,b_1+1)\\) and \\((i,b_2-1)\\), both of which belong to \\(P_i\\) because \\(b_1+1\\) and \\(b_2-1\\) are still between \\(1\\) and \\(n+1-i\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell_i\\) is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\), it is sunny.\n\nEvery point of \\(P_i\\) lies on the segment joining \\((i,b_1+1)\\) and \\((i,b_2-1)\\); consequently it also lies on the line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nHence we may **replace** the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) without losing any coverage of points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nJ\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad |J|=k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in J\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) constructed in §2; for \\(i\\notin J\\) keep the original vertical line \\(x=i\\).  \nTogether with the unchanged horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k\n=\n\\bigl\\{\\,\\ell_i\\mid i\\in J\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\notin J\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the lines indexed by \\(J\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy the argument of §2 each point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered only by a replaced vertical line is still covered by the corresponding sunny line, while all other points remain covered by the unchanged lines.  \nTherefore every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nSince \\(J\\) can be any subset of size \\(k\\), we have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n> (i) each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThe existence (or impossibility) of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) remains an open question. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
