            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nWe prove that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists **iff** the number of sunny lines is either \\(0\\) or \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for the extreme values  \n\n**(i)  \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n**(ii)  \\(k=n\\).**  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(0<\\alpha<1\\) (so \\(\\alpha\\neq 0,-1\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=\\alpha x + (c-\\alpha c). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  If \\(c\\neq c'\\) then the right‑hand sides of (1) differ, hence \\(L_c\\neq L_{c'}\\).\n\n*Sunny property.*  The slope of every \\(L_c\\) is \\(\\alpha\\); because \\(\\alpha\\neq 0\\) and \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\), the line is not parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\), \\(\\ell_y\\) nor \\(\\ell_d\\).  Hence each \\(L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique integer \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  Substituting \\(b=c-a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nc-a = \\alpha a + (c-\\alpha c)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nb = \\alpha a + (c-\\alpha c),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of \\(L_c\\).  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Non‑existence for intermediate numbers of sunny lines  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{S}\\subset\\mathcal{F}\\) be the set of sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal{S}|=k\\)) and \\(\\mathcal{N}=\\mathcal{F}\\setminus\\mathcal{S}\\) the set of non‑sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal{N}|=n-k\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(a_i\\) are not all equal; similarly they are not all distinct with the same difference because that would force the slope to be \\(-1\\).  Computing the slope from any two of the points gives a value different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Using the three points to solve for the slope yields the equation  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1},\n\\]\n\nwhich after clearing denominators simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n(b_2-b_1)(a_3-a_1)=(b_3-b_1)(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nSince each \\((a_i,b_i)\\) satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the only way the above identity can hold for three distinct lattice points is when the common slope equals \\(-1\\); but a slope \\(-1\\) would make \\(\\ell\\) parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  Hence at most two points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  A non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\n\n* If it is horizontal, its equation is \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\le n\\); thus at most \\(n\\) lattice points satisfy both \\(y=c\\) and the inequality.\n\n* If it is vertical, the argument is symmetric.\n\n* If it is parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\), its equation can be written \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\); for each admissible \\(c\\) the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\) are at most \\(c-1\\le n\\).  \n\nHence any non‑sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points that can be covered by the family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\):\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal{F}}\\ell\\cap T_n\\bigr|\n\\le (n-k)\\cdot n + k\\cdot 2 = n^{2}-kn+2k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the left‑hand side of (2) is at least \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn+2k \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and rearranging yields\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-(2k-1)n+4k \\ge 0 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider the quadratic polynomial \\(p(k)=n^{2}-(2k-1)n+4k\\) as a function of \\(k\\) on the interval \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Its discriminant is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = (2k-1)^{2}-16k = 4k^{2}-4k+1-16k = 4k^{2}-20k+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have \\(k\\le n-1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\); a direct check shows \\(\\Delta<0\\) (indeed the quadratic \\(4k^{2}-20k+1\\) attains its maximum at \\(k=2.5\\) with value \\(-24\\)).  Therefore the left‑hand side of (3) is **strictly negative** for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\), contradicting (3).\n\nConsequently no configuration with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe covering condition can be satisfied with\n\n* \\(k=0\\) sunny lines (the vertical‑line construction), and  \n* \\(k=n\\) sunny lines (the family \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) defined in (1)).\n\nFor any other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) the required configuration is impossible.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
