            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_c\\) form a partition of \\(T_n\\) and  \n\n\\[\n|D_c|=c-1\\qquad(2\\le c\\le n+1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions  \n\\(\\ell_x:y=0,\\;\\ell_y:x=0,\\;\\ell_d:x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for the extreme values  \n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).  \nHence the family \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha=\\tfrac12\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=\\alpha x+(c-\\alpha c). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\alpha\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore every \\(L_c\\) is sunny.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(c=a+b\\) (so \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\)).  \nThen \\(b=c-a\\), and substituting into (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nc-a=\\alpha a+(c-\\alpha c),\n\\]\n\nwhich is an identity; consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (2) with that \\(c\\) and lies on \\(L_c\\).  \nThe lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) are distinct because the constant term \\(c-\\alpha c\\) differs for different \\(c\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_c\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  No configuration with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(d\\) = number of lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) that are parallel to the diagonal \\(\\ell_d:x+y=0\\).  \n  Such lines are non‑sunny, so \\(0\\le d\\le n-k\\).\n\n* The remaining \\(n-d\\) lines are **non‑diagonal** (they are either vertical, horizontal, or have a slope different from \\(-1\\)).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA non‑diagonal line meets each antidiagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  If two distinct points of the same \\(D_c\\) lay on the line, their slope would be \\(-1\\), contradicting the hypothesis that the line is not parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\). ∎\n\nConsequences of Lemma 1:\n\n* A diagonal line can cover an entire antidiagonal.\n* A non‑diagonal line can contribute **at most one** point to each antidiagonal.\n\nThe \\(d\\) diagonal lines can therefore cover at most \\(d\\) whole antidiagonals.  \nChoose them to cover the \\(d\\) largest antidiagonals, namely \\(D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n+2-d}\\).  \nAll remaining antidiagonals are  \n\n\\[\nD_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1-d}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (1), the total number of points that still have to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=2}^{\\,n+1-d}(c-1)=\\frac{(n-d)(n-d+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach of the \\(n-d\\) non‑diagonal lines can supply at most one point to each of the \\((n-d)\\) antidiagonals listed above; therefore a single non‑diagonal line can cover at most \\((n-d)\\) of the points counted in (3).  \nConsequently we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-d)(n-d+1)}{2}\\Big/ (n-d)=\\frac{n-d+1}{2}\n\\]\n\nnon‑diagonal lines.  Since there are exactly \\(n-d\\) non‑diagonal lines, we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-d\\ge\\frac{n-d+1}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n-d\\ge n-d+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible unless the denominator in the division is zero, i.e. unless the set of points (3) is empty.  The set (3) is empty precisely when \\(d=n\\).\n\nThus the only way to satisfy the covering requirement with \\(n\\) lines is to have \\(d=n\\) (all lines diagonal) or \\(d=0\\) (no diagonal line).  \n\n* If \\(d=n\\) then every line is parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\); none is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n* If \\(d=0\\) then every line is non‑diagonal.  By Lemma 1 each such line meets each antidiagonal in at most one point, so to cover the antidiagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) of size \\(n\\) we need at least \\(n\\) distinct non‑diagonal lines.  Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of them must be non‑diagonal and each must be used to cover a distinct point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  Hence all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\).\n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe problem admits a solution exactly for  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n .\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable by the constructions given in Section 1, and any intermediate value of \\(k\\) leads to a contradiction. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
