thcal L_0\\) are parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n1. **If \\(a=1\\).** Then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n2. **If \\(a\\ge 2\\).** Then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\).  \n   - If \\(b\\le n-2\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n   - If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a=2\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and the point \\((2,n-1)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\); consequently it lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\in\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). ∎\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\\(n-2\\) horizontals, one diagonal, and one vertical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing horizontal lines by sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains the lattice points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAmong these points the leftmost one, \\((1,j)\\), is *uniquely* covered by the line \\(y=j\\): it does **not** belong to any other line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) because the only other lines are \\(x=1\\) (which contains \\((1,b)\\) for all \\(b\\)) and \\(x+y=n\\) (which contains points with coordinates summing to \\(n\\)). Since \\(1+j\\le n\\) for \\(j\\le n-1\\), the point \\((1,j)\\) is not on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\); it is on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) only when \\(j=1\\), which we have excluded. Hence for each \\(j\\ge 2\\) the point \\((1,j)\\) is covered **only** by the horizontal line \\(y=j\\).\n\nConsequently, for any subset \\(J\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we may delete the horizontals \\(\\{y