points of the triangle that are not on any other line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\). Consequently at least one of them must stay in any covering family; otherwise some lattice point would become uncovered. Hence at most \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny, proving that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable and no larger \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering by \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\underbrace{\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x+y=n\\}}_{\\text{slope }-1}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x=1\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\).\n\n- If \\(b\\le n-2\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\).\n- If \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a=2\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and \\(a+b=n+1\\); consequently the point \\((2,n-1)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n\\) and lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\).\n\nThus every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\). \u220e\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\\(n-2\\) horizontals, one diagonal, and one vertical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lines that can be removed without losing coverage\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) consider the horizontal line \\(y=j\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nAmong the points of this line that belong to \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,j),\\;(2,j),\\dots ,\\bigl(n+1-j,j\\bigr).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe leftmost point \\((1,j)\\) is **uniquely** covered by the line \\(y=j\\):\n- It is not on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) because that line contains points with any \\(y\\), but \\((1,j)\\) already belongs to \\(x=1\\); however the point \\((1,j)\\) *is* on \\(x=1\\).  \n  The crucial observation is that the only other line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) that could contain \\((1,j)\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \n- Since \\(1+j\\le n\\) for \\(j\\le n-1\\), we have \\(1+j\\neq n\\); therefore \\((1,j)\\) is **not** on the diagonal.\n\nHence \\((1,j)\\) is covered by exactly the two lines \\(y=j\\) and \\(x=1\\); but the point \\((1,j)\\) with \\(j\\ge 2\\) is already covered by \\(x=1\\). The important fact is that **no other horizontal line of \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains \\((1,j)\\)**, so when we delete the line \\(y=j\\) the only points of that line that might become uncovered are those not already on \\(x=1\\) or on the diagonal. All such points are covered by either \\(x=1\\) (the points with abscissa \\(1\\)) or by the diagonal (the points with abscissa \\(n+1-j\\)). Consequently, after deleting \\(y=j\\) we can restore coverage of the unique point \\((1,j)\\) by inserting any new line that passes through \\((1,j)\\).\n\nThus each horizontal line \\(y=j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) can be removed and replaced by a new line through \\((1,j)\\) without destroying the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction of families with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose an arbitrary subset  \n\n\\[\nJ\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad |J|=k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\in J\\) select a line \\(\\ell_{j}\\) that\n\n* passes through the point \\((1,j)\\);\n* has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nSuch a line is sunny by definition.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{y=j\\mid j\\in J\\}\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\ell_{j}\\mid j\\in J\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Properties of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).**\n\n1. **Cardinality.**  \n   We removed \\(|J|=k\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and added exactly \\(k\\) new lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=n\\).\n\n2. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   The only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) are the newly inserted \\(\\ell_{j}\\) (\\(j\\in J\\)); hence \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Covering property.**  \n   - Points that were on a horizontal line \\(y=j\\) with \\(j\\notin J\\) remain covered, because those horizontals are still present.  \n   - For a deleted horizontal \\(y=j\\) (\\(j\\in J\\)), every point of that line except \\((1,j)\\) is already on either \\(x=1\\) (the points with abscissa \\(1\\)) or on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) (the point with abscissa \\(n+1-j\\)). The point \\((1,j)\\) is now covered by the newly added sunny line \\(\\ell_{j}\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Because the choice of \\(J\\) was arbitrary, such a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal M\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nSince at most three lines can be non\u2011sunny (parallel to the three forbidden directions), \\(\\mathcal M\\) would contain at most one non\u2011sunny line.\n\nRecall that in any covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines, the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,n-1)\\) must be covered.  \nThe point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the two forbidden directions \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); if neither of these two lines is present, the only way to cover \\((1,1)\\) is by a sunny line passing through it.  \nSimilarly, the point \\((1,n-1)\\) is on the forbidden line \\(x=1\\) but not on any other forbidden line; if \\(x=1\\) is absent, a sunny line must pass through \\((1,n-1)\\).\n\nNow consider the set of points on the leftmost column of the triangle,\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\nIf the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is missing, each point of \\(C\\) must be covered by a distinct sunny line (different slopes are required because two distinct sunny lines cannot share two points, otherwise they would coincide). That would already require at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, leaving no room for any other line, contradicting the fact that the points on the bottom row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}\\) also need to be covered. A symmetric argument applies if the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is missing.\n\nHence at least one of the two lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) must belong to any covering family of size \\(n\\). Consequently at most \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny. Therefore configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) do **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBoth existence (by the explicit construction of Section\u202f3) and impossibility of larger values (by the argument of Section\u202f4) have been proved rigorously. \u220e"
