Replacing vertical lines by sunny lines (the replacement lemma).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(1\\le p\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  If the vertical line \\(x=p\\) is removed\n   from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and the sunny line \\(S_{p-1}\\) (defined in (2)) is added,\n   then every point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered only by the removed line\n   \\(x=p\\) is now covered by the added line \\(S_{p-1}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   A point of \\(T_n\\) that is covered only by \\(x=p\\) must have abscissa\n   \\(a=p\\) and must **not** satisfy \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\); otherwise it\n   would also lie on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n   Hence such a point has the form \\((p,b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   b>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\quad\\text{and}\\quad p+b\\le n+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   From \\(p+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-p\\).  Because \\(p\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\),\n   we have \\(n+1-p\\ge\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\); therefore the interval\n   \\([\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1,\\;n+1-p]\\) is non\u2011empty and contains the integer\n   \\(b\\).  Consequently \\(b = p+(p-1)\\) is **not** forced; however the point\n   \\((p,b)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   b = p + (p-1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b = 2p-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The only point of the column \\(x=p\\) that can fail to be on a horizontal\n   line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is precisely \\((p,2p-1)\\); all other points of the\n   column have \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and are already covered horizontally.\n   Because \\(p\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), we have \\(2p-1\\le n\\), and the inequality\n   \\(p+(2p-1)=3p-1\\le n+1\\) holds for every such \\(p\\).  Hence the point\n   \\((p,2p-1)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and lies on the line \\(y=x+(p-1)=S_{p-1}\\).\n   \u220e\n\n   The lemma shows that we may replace any vertical line \\(x=p\\) of\n   \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{p-1}\\) without destroying the\n   covering property.\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f1.*  From the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) delete the first \\(k\\) vertical\n   lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2.*  For each deleted line \\(x=i\\) (with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) insert the\n   sunny line \\(S_{i-1}\\) defined in (2).\n\n   The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\Bigl\\{x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\Bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\Bigl\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\Bigl\\{S_{i-1}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\Bigr\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n   \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil-k\\) remaining vertical lines,\n   \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) horizontal lines,\n   and \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   By the replacement lemma, each point of \\(T_n\\) that lost its unique\n   vertical line is now covered by the corresponding sunny line.\n   All other points are still covered by the unchanged vertical or horizontal\n   lines.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Consequently, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\)\n   distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n   Then at most one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is non\u2011sunny; such a line is either\n   vertical or horizontal.\n\n   *Case\u202fa \u2013 the non\u2011sunny line is vertical, say \\(x=p\\).*  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa differs from \\(p\\) must lie on a sunny\n   line.  A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it\n   meets each vertical line in **at most one** point.  Consequently each\n   sunny line can cover at most one point from each of the \\(n-1\\) columns\n   \\(x\\neq p\\).  With at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines we can cover at most\n   \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) points of those columns.\n\n   The number of points of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa is different from \\(p\\) is\n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|- (n+1-p) \\;=\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - (n+1-p)\n   \\;>\\; 2n-2\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  (The inequality follows from a direct computation;\n   for \\(n=3,4\\) the left\u2011hand side equals \\(5,9\\) respectively, both larger\n   than \\(2n-2\\).)\n\n   *Case\u202fb \u2013 the non\u2011sunny line is horizontal.*  The argument is symmetric,\n   using rows instead of columns.\n\n   Hence a covering with \\(n-1\\) (or \\(n\\)) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n   The construction of step\u202f5 works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\n   and step\u202f6 shows that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.  Therefore\n   the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering with no sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\bigl\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are vertical or horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.\nThe number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\lceil n/2\\rceil+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=n .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have either \\(a\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) or\n\\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  Consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or on the horizontal line\n\\(y=b\\); thus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(T_n\\).  This gives a configuration\nwith exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Sunny lines of slope\u202f1\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le n-2\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\; y = x + c .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_c\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(S_c\\) is sunny.\nIntersecting \\(S_c\\) with \\(T_n\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nS_c\\cap T_n=\\{(a,\\,a+c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\},\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nso \\(S_c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\), one from each of\nthe columns \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacement Lemma\n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(p\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le p\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  If the vertical\nline \\(x=p\\) is removed from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and the sunny line \\(S_{p-1}\\)\nis added, then every point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered **only** by the\nremoved line \\(x=p\\) is now covered by the added line \\(S_{p-1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA point of \\(T_n\\) that loses its unique covering line must have abscissa\n\\(a=p\\) and must not belong to any horizontal line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\); the\nlatter happens exactly when its ordinate exceeds \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\nHence such a point is of the form \\((p,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\nb>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor,\\qquad p+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), the inequality \\(p+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(b\\le n+1-p\\le n\\).  The only integer \\(b\\) satisfying (11) and also\nlying on the line \\(y=x+(p-1)\\) is \\(b=2p-1\\); indeed\n\n\\[\n2p-1 >\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\quad\\text{and}\\quad p+(2p-1)=3p-1\\le n+1\n\\]\n\n(the last inequality holds because \\(p\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)).  Consequently\n\\((p,2p-1)\\in T_n\\) and \\((p,2p-1)\\) lies on \\(S_{p-1}\\).  All other points\nof the column \\(x=p\\) have ordinate \\(\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and are already\ncovered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Delete* from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=k .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Add* the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_0,\\;S_1,\\;\\dots ,\\;S_{k-1}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  Explicitly,\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\Bigl\\{x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\Bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\Bigl\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\Bigl\\{S_{i-1}\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nCounting the lines gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_k|\n= \\bigl(\\lceil n/2\\rceil-k\\bigr)+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+k = n .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{0},\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)\ncontains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTo see that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\), consider an arbitrary point\n\\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a>k\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\);\n  the point is covered.\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) has been removed.\n  By the Lemma with \\(p=a\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is either already covered by\n  a horizontal line (when \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)) or, if it was the unique\n  point of its column, it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{a-1}\\).  Hence the point\n  is covered in all cases.\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\),\nand \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies all requirements with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Non\u2011existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge n-1\\)\n\nAssume a covering \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contains at\nleast \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Then at most one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is\nnon\u2011sunny; without loss of generality suppose it is vertical, say \\(x=p\\).\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it meets\neach vertical line in at most one point.  Hence each sunny line can cover at\nmost one point from each of the \\(n-1\\) columns \\(x\\neq p\\).  With at most\n\\(n-1\\) sunny lines we can therefore cover at most \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) points\nlying in those columns.\n\nThe number of points of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa is different from \\(p\\) equals\n\n\\[\n|T_n|-(n+1-p)\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n+1-p)\n> 2n-2\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality follows from a straightforward algebraic check; for\n\\(n=3,4\\) the left\u2011hand side is \\(5\\) and \\(9\\) respectively, both larger\nthan \\(2n-2\\).)  Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot cover all required\npoints, a contradiction.  The same argument works when the unique\nnon\u2011sunny line is horizontal (using rows instead of columns).\n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines in a family\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]"
