## 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\n> **Lemma 3.1 (Replacement).**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a family of distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \n> Suppose \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\).  \n> Then there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that the family  \n> \\(\\displaystyle \\mathcal C'=(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\\) also covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny, its slope is either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nIn every case the intersection \\(L\\cap T_n\\) is a non\u2011empty segment whose endpoints are lattice points of \\(T_n\\). Moreover, **each point of this segment is incident with at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) different from \\(L\\)**.  \nIndeed, this property holds for the initial family \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) (see the verification above):  \n\n* on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) also lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+1\\);\n* on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) the point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) also lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=b+1\\);\n* on a diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) the two endpoints \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\) lie on the vertical and horizontal lines respectively.\n\nWhen we replace a line later in the induction, the same statement remains true: any point that was on the removed line was already covered by another line, because the only way a point could have become *uniquely* covered by that line would be if we had previously removed **all** other lines passing through it, which never happens (the replacement step always keeps at least one other line through each such point). Hence the inductive hypothesis guarantees the property for the current family \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\nChoose any point \\(P\\in L\\cap T_n\\). Because of the property just proved, there exists another line \\(M\\in\\mathcal C\\), \\(M\\neq L\\), with \\(P\\in M\\).\n\nSelect a slope \\(\\mu\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\mu\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\mu\\neq\\text{slope of any line in }\\mathcal C .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(\\mu\\) exists because the set of forbidden slopes is finite while the real numbers are infinite.  \n\nLet \\(S\\) be the unique line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\mu\\). By construction \\(S\\) is sunny and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\nNow consider any point \\(Q\\in L\\cap T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(Q=P\\) then \\(Q\\) lies on the new line \\(S\\).\n* If \\(Q\\neq P\\) then, by the inductive property, \\(Q\\) lies on some line \\(N\\in\\mathcal C\\) with \\(N\\neq L\\). Since \\(N\\) is retained in \\(\\mathcal C'\\), the point \\(Q\\) remains covered.\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by \\(\\mathcal C\\) is still covered by \\(\\mathcal C'\\). The family \\(\\mathcal C'\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines (we removed one and added a new one) and contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal C\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nStart with the base family \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) from \u00a72, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines and satisfies the covering condition.\n\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done.  \nAssume we have already constructed a family \\(\\mathcal C_r\\) with \\(r\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le r<n\\)) that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nSince \\(r<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_r\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nApply Lemma\u202f3.1 to that line; we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal C_{r+1}\\) that still covers \\(T_n\\) and now has \\(r+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n\\) we can produce a family \\(\\mathcal C_r\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (1) and the constructive procedure of \u00a74 together show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
