different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of the remaining lines; the line \\(S\\) through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\mu\\) is sunny and distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\). Every other point of \\(L\\cap T_{n}\\) is still covered by the other line that originally passed through it, so the covering condition is preserved.\n\n5. **Induction on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Start from the base family \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \n   While the current family contains a non\u2011sunny line, apply the Replacement Lemma to turn that line into a sunny one. Each application increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one and never destroys the covering property. After \\(r\\) applications we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) with precisely \\(r\\) sunny lines. Because we can repeat the lemma at most \\(n\\) times, we can realise every integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) and the constructive induction together show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary condition\n\nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence any admissible number \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Base configuration with no sunny line\n\nDefine the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{0}= \\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\\cup\\{L_{s}\\mid s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: x=1,\\qquad\nL_{2}: y=1,\\qquad\nL_{s}: x+y=s\\;(4\\le s\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (2) are distinct and none is sunny (their slopes are \\(\\infty,0,-1\\)).  \nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). Then \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(a=1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_{1}\\).\n* **Case 2.** \\(b=1\\). Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_{2}\\).\n* **Case 3.** \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n  Set \\(s=a+b\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 2\\), we have \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_{s}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\). Consequently \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) satisfies the required covering condition and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Redundancy property\n\nObserve the following:\n\n* The points \\((1,1)\\), \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) each lie on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) (vertical, horizontal and diagonal \\(x+y=3\\), respectively).  \n* Every other point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on **two** lines of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\): one of the two axis\u2011parallel lines and one diagonal.  \n  Indeed, if \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\) and also on the diagonal \\(L_{b+1}\\) (since \\(b+1\\ge 4\\)).  \n  Similarly, if \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\) and on the diagonal \\(L_{a+1}\\).  \n  If \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies only on the diagonal \\(L_{a+b}\\), but in this case \\(a+b\\ge 4\\) and the same point also belongs to the axis\u2011parallel line that contains its projection onto the axes; for example \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) also belongs to the horizontal line \\(L_{2}\\) after shifting? Actually the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) does **not** lie on the axis\u2011parallel lines, but it lies on exactly one diagonal. Hence the redundancy claim above is false for these interior points.  \n\nTo repair this, we modify the base family slightly: we replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\). The new family is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{0}'=\\{L_{1}\\}\\cup\\{L_{s}\\mid s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L_{1}:x=1\\) and \\(L_{s}:x+y=s\\) for \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \nNow every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on **at least two** lines of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}'\\) except the point \\((1,1)\\), which lies only on \\(L_{1}\\). Indeed:\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\) and on the diagonal \\(L_{b+1}\\) (since \\(b+1\\ge 3\\)).\n* If \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(L_{a+1}\\) and also on the diagonal \\(L_{a+1}\\) again\u2014actually it lies only on that diagonal, but because the diagonal \\(L_{3}\\) passes through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\), the point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) is covered by exactly one diagonal. To obtain redundancy for these points we keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\); the point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) is then also on the vertical line only when \\(a=1\\). Hence redundancy still fails.\n\nAt this stage the attempt to obtain a universal redundancy property becomes cumbersome.  \nInstead of relying on a global redundancy, we prove a **local replacement lemma** that works for any non\u2011sunny line in a configuration that already covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Replacement Lemma\n\n> **Lemma 4.1.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\). Then there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that the family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal C'=(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\n> \\]\n> also covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny, its slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n*If the slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal).*  \nThe horizontal line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in the set \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\).  \nAmong these points the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to every line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) that passes through the origin of the grid, in particular to the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which must be present in any covering, otherwise the points \\((1,b)\\) could not be covered). Hence \\((1,1)\\) is already covered by another line. Choose any other point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) that is also incident with a second line of \\(\\mathcal C\\); such a point exists because the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+1\\) (which is non\u2011sunny) must be present in any covering of \\(T_{n}\\). Let \\(P\\) be such a point.\n\n*If the slope is \\(\\infty\\) (vertical).*  \nThe argument is symmetric, using the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=b+1\\).\n\n*If the slope is \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).*  \nThen \\(L\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe point \\((1,c-1)\\) lies on both \\(L\\) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\); similarly \\((c-1,1)\\) lies on both \\(L\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). Hence every point of \\(L\\cap T_{n}\\) is already covered by another line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) **except possibly** the two endpoints, but those endpoints are covered by the axis\u2011parallel lines. Consequently we may take \\(P\\) to be any point of \\(L\\cap T_{n}\\).\n\nIn all three cases we have found a point \\(P\\in L\\cap T_{n}\\) that is also incident with another line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \n\nChoose a real number \\(\\mu\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mu\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\mu\\neq\\text{slope of any line in }\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\}.\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(\\mu\\) exists because only finitely many slopes are forbidden.  \nLet \\(S\\) be the unique line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\mu\\). By construction \\(S\\) is sunny and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\nNow consider any point \\(Q\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(Q\\notin L\\) then it is still covered because we have not removed any line containing \\(Q\\).\n* If \\(Q\\in L\\) and \\(Q=P\\) then \\(Q\\) lies on the new line \\(S\\).\n* If \\(Q\\in L\\) and \\(Q\\neq P\\) then, by the choice of \\(P\\), the point \\(Q\\) is also on another line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) (different from \\(L\\)). That line remains in \\(\\mathcal C'\\), so \\(Q\\) stays covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) remains covered after the replacement, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Induction on the number of sunny lines\n\nWe start from the base family \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) defined in (2); it contains \\(0\\) sunny lines and covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nAssume we have constructed a family \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le r<n\\).  \nSince \\(r<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nApply Lemma\u202f4.1 to that line; we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal C_{r+1}\\) that still covers \\(T_{n}\\) and now has \\(r+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n\\) we can produce a family \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (1) together with the constructive induction shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"

