cally \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\); its points lie on\n\\(n\\) rows (constant \\(b\\)) and on \\(n\\) columns (constant \\(a\\)).  \nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists i\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell_i,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny*, i.e. are **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=0).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  An unavoidable pair of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nIn any family of lines satisfying (3) at least one line is parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. has slope \\(-1\\)) and at least one line is parallel to either\nthe \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Diagonal direction.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to the forbidden diagonal \\(x+y=0\\).  \nIf the family does **not** contain a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), then each\nline can intersect \\(d\\) in at most one point (otherwise two distinct points\nof \\(d\\) would determine a line parallel to \\(d\\)).  \nSince \\(d\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), covering them would\nrequire at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, contradicting the hypothesis that we\nhave only \\(n\\) lines in total (the other points of \\(T_n\\) would need additional\nlines).  Hence a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is mandatory.\n\n*Horizontal/vertical direction.*  \nConsider the points of the first row  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf the family contains no horizontal line, then any line that meets two\ndifferent points of \\(R_1\\) would have to be horizontal, which is forbidden.\nConsequently each line can contain **at most one** point of \\(R_1\\).  \nSince \\(|R_1|=n\\), covering all points of \\(R_1\\) would again need at least\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, leaving no line for the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus a horizontal line is necessary.  (The same argument works with the\nfirst column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) and forces a vertical line if we\nchoose not to use a horizontal one.)\n\nTherefore any admissible family must contain at least one line parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) and at least one line parallel to the \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-axis. \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 we obtain the **upper bound**\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed the two mandatory lines are not sunny, so at most the remaining\n\\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that the bound (7) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  A base configuration with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nDefine the following three lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &:\\; x+y=n+1 \\qquad\\text{(parallel to }x+y=0\\text{)},\\\\[2pt]\nh &:\\; y=1          \\qquad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\[2pt]\nv &:\\; x=1          \\qquad\\text{(vertical)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by the union of these three lines:\n\n* points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(d\\);\n* points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(h\\);\n* points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(v\\);\n* any remaining point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and\n  \\(a+b\\le n\\); then either \\(a\\le n-1\\) or \\(b\\le n-1\\), so it lies on the\n  vertical line \\(x=a\\) or on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{d,h,v\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\).  The three lines are distinct and exactly two of them\n(\\(d\\) and whichever of \\(h\\) or \\(v\\) we keep) are non\u2011sunny; the third one\nmay be taken to be either horizontal or vertical, whichever is convenient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Adding auxiliary lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) introduce the two lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_t &: y=t \\quad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\nV_t &: x=t \\quad\\text{(vertical)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny.  Observe that for any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\nwith \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\) or \\(b\\le n-1\\); consequently the point\nlies on at least one of the lines \\(H_b\\) or \\(V_a\\).  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{d,h,v\\}\\cup\\{H_t,V_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\).  The cardinality of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal F| = 3 + 2\\,(n-2)=2n-1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe start from the set \\(\\mathcal F\\) and keep the two mandatory non\u2011sunny\nlines \\(d\\) and \\(h\\) (the diagonal and the horizontal \\(y=1\\)).  \nAll remaining \\(2n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny horizontals or verticals.\nWe shall replace a suitable number of them by sunny lines while preserving\nthe covering property.\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_k\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) pick a point \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) that **does not** lie on the\ndiagonal \\(d\\) nor on the horizontal line \\(h\\); for instance we may take\n\\((a_i,b_i)=(i+1,2)\\) (these points are distinct because \\(i\\) ranges from\n\\(1\\) to \\(k\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\alpha_i x + (b_i-\\alpha_i a_i).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and has an admissible\nslope, hence it is sunny.\n\nNow delete from \\(\\mathcal F\\) the \\(k\\) non\u2011sunny lines that originally\ncovered the chosen points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) (each such point lies on a unique\nhorizontal line \\(y=b_i\\) and on a unique vertical line \\(x=a_i\\); we delete\none of the two).  Replace them by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains the two mandatory non\u2011sunny lines \\(d\\) and \\(h\\),\nthe remaining \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny horizontals/verticals, and the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C_k| = 2 + (n-2-k) + k = n .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n*Covering property.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by a line we did **not** delete\nremains covered.  Each deleted non\u2011sunny line is replaced by a sunny line\nthat passes through the same point \\((a_i,b_i)\\); therefore that point is\nstill covered.  No other point loses its covering because each deleted line\ncontained at most one point that is not already covered by another line\n(the other points of the same horizontal/vertical line are covered by the\ncorresponding vertical/horizontal line that we kept).  Consequently all\npoints of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny; all the other\n\\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to one of the forbidden directions.  Hence\n\\(\\mathcal C_k\\) satisfies the required condition with precisely \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all\nthese values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f2.1 shows that any admissible family must contain at least two\nnon\u2011sunny lines, whence \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction of Section\u202f3 produces, for each integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\ncovering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family\nexists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}}.\n\\]"
