            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A necessary condition: at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad \n\\text{In any family }\\mathcal L\\text{ of }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }T_n,\n\\text{ at least two lines are non‑sunny. Consequently }k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(j,1)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\); therefore it meets the horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) in **at most one** point. Hence each point of \\(B\\) must lie on a line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) that is either horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (parallel\nto \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nThus at least \\(n\\) incidences of the set \\(B\\) with lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are\nrealised by lines of the two non‑sunny directions. Since a line can contain at\nmost one point of \\(B\\) unless it is horizontal, the only way to use fewer than\n\\(n\\) non‑sunny lines is to have some vertical (sunny) lines that each contain a\nsingle point of \\(B\\). A vertical line can contain at most one point of \\(B\\) as\nwell, so the total number of lines needed to cover \\(B\\) is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{horizontal or slope }-1\\text{ lines}\\}+ \\#\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\}\n\\ge n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow look at the leftmost column  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(1,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning shows that each point of \\(L\\) must lie on a line that\nis either vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{vertical or slope }-1\\text{ lines}\\}+ \\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\}\n\\ge n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdd (1) and (2). The lines with slope \\(-1\\) are counted twice, while the\nhorizontal and vertical lines are each counted once. Denoting by\n\\(h,v,d\\) the numbers of horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines in\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(h+v+d)+(h+v+d)\\ge 2n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad h+v+d\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the number of sunny lines, which are precisely the lines that are\nneither horizontal, vertical nor slope \\(-1\\), is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(h+v+d)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe only way to have any sunny line is to replace one of the three\nnon‑sunny directions by a sunny one, but then the corresponding inequality\n(1) or (2) would be violated because a sunny line can cover at most one point\nof the respective row or column. Consequently at least **two** of the three\ndirections must appear among the \\(n\\) lines, i.e. at least two lines are\nnon‑sunny. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 2.1. Sunny lines\n\nThe diagonal \\(D\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points. Choose any \\(k\\) distinct\npoints on \\(D\\); write them as  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(a_i,b_i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) select a slope \\(m_i\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\},\n\\]\n\nand define the line  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=m_i x+(b_i-m_i a_i).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(s_i\\) passes through \\(Q_i\\) and is sunny. By taking the slopes\n\\(m_i\\) pairwise distinct we guarantee that the lines \\(s_i\\) are distinct.\n\n#### 2.2. Non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\n\\]\nwhich are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=k+2,\n\\]\nwhich is horizontal. Both kinds are non‑sunny.\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k-1}\\}\\cup\\{H\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k-1)+1=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 2.3. Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a+b=n+1\\) (the point lies on the diagonal \\(D\\)).*  \n\nIf \\((a,b)=Q_i\\) for some \\(i\\), then \\((a,b)\\in s_i\\).  \nOtherwise \\((a,b)\\neq Q_i\\) for all \\(i\\). Because the only horizontal line in\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) is \\(y=k+2\\), we have \\(b\\neq k+2\\). Hence \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) and the\npoint lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a+b<n+1\\).*  \n\nIf \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge n-k\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k+1\\).  \n\n- If \\(b=k+2\\) we would have \\(a=n-k\\) and the point would be \\((n-k,k+2)\\),\n  which lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).  \n- Otherwise \\(b\\le k+1\\); set \\(a' = n+1-b\\). Then \\(a'\\ge n-k\\) and\n  \\((a',b)\\in D\\). Since \\((a',b)\\neq Q_i\\) for all \\(i\\) (otherwise we would\n  already be in Case 1), we have \\(a'\\le n-k-1\\) and consequently\n  \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (because \\(a\\le a'\\) and both\n  share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate). In any sub‑case the point is covered.\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists. Therefore the set of all\nnon‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of \\(n\\) lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
