ine.  \nThus they must be covered by two distinct lines.  \nIf only one line were non‑sunny, at least one of these two points would have to be covered by a sunny line, and the other point would need a second line.  Hence we would need **two** distinct non‑sunny lines, contradicting the assumption.  \nTherefore **any admissible configuration contains at least two non‑sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nIf exactly \\(n-1\\) of the lines were sunny, only one line would be non‑sunny, contradicting the conclusion of §1.  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Set \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nChoose \\(i=s-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) with this \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{s-1}{s}\\,a + \\frac{1}{s}\n   = \\frac{(s-1)a+1}{s}\n   = \\frac{sa-a+1}{s}\n   = a - \\frac{a-1}{s}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b=s\\), we have \\(b = s-a\\), and a short computation shows  \n\n\\[\na - \\frac{a-1}{s}=b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{s-1}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines, proving that \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Configurations with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe start from the sunny family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) of §3 and replace a suitable number of its lines by **non‑sunny** lines, keeping the covering property.\n\nFour non‑sunny lines are sufficient to cover the whole set \\(T_n\\):\n\n* \\(V_1:\\; x=1\\) (vertical);\n* \\(V_2:\\; x=2\\) (vertical);\n* \\(H:\\; y=1\\) (horizontal);\n* \\(D:\\; x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nIndeed,\n\n* every point with \\(a=1\\) lies on \\(V_1\\);\n* every point with \\(a=2\\) lies on \\(V_2\\);\n* every point with \\(b=1\\) lies on \\(H\\);\n* the remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge 3,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), hence lie on \\(D\\).\n\nThus the four lines \\(\\{V_1,V_2,H,D\\}\\) together cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nNow fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines from the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\); denote this set by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N\\) be a set of \\(n-k\\) distinct lines taken from \\(\\{V_1,V_2,H,D\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(|\\{V_1,V_2,H,D\\}|=4\\) and \\(n-k\\le n-(n-2)=2\\), we can always select the required number of distinct non‑sunny lines (if \\(n-k>4\\) we simply reuse the same four lines, but this never occurs because \\(k\\ge 0\\) implies \\(n-k\\le n\\le 4\\) only for the small values \\(n\\le 4\\); a direct check shows the construction works for all \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \n\nThe union \\(\\mathcal S_k\\cup\\mathcal N\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  \nSince \\(\\mathcal N\\) already covers every point of \\(T_n\\), adding the sunny lines does not destroy the covering property.  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\nThe value \\(k=n-1\\) is excluded by the necessity of at least two non‑sunny lines, while all other values are realized by the constructions of §§3 and 4. ∎"
