            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Two non‑sunny lines are unavoidable  \n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of lines satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists \\ell\\in\\mathcal L:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and at\nleast one line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (or the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Diagonal.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no line parallel\nto \\(x+y=0\\), then each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) could intersect \\(d\\) in at most\none point; consequently covering the \\(n\\) points of \\(d\\cap T_n\\) would need\nat least \\(n\\) distinct lines, leaving no line for the remaining points of\n\\(T_n\\).  Hence a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Horizontal (or vertical).*  \nConsider the first row  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no horizontal line, then any line meeting two\ndifferent points of \\(R_1\\) would have to be horizontal, a contradiction.\nThus each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain at most one point of \\(R_1\\),\nand covering all \\(n\\) points of \\(R_1\\) would again require at least \\(n\\)\nlines, which is impossible.  Therefore a horizontal line is mandatory\n(the same argument works with the first column and yields a vertical line if\nwe prefer a vertical one).\n\n∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 2.1 we obtain the **necessary inequality**\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed the two lines guaranteed by the lemma are not sunny, so at most the\nremaining \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A base family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nDefine the following three lines:\n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1,\\qquad\nh:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nv_t:\\;x=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n-2).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{d,h\\}\\cup\\{v_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal B\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal B|=2+(n-3)=n .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal B\\) are distinct and non‑sunny (the first two are\nparallel to the forbidden directions, the verticals are parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis).\n\n**Claim.**  \\(\\mathcal B\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in h\\).\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(a\\le n-2\\) is impossible because \\(a=1\\); nevertheless\n  \\((1,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d\\) when \\(b=n\\) and otherwise on the\n  horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which coincides with \\(h\\) only for \\(b=1\\).  In\n  the latter case \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n\\); the\n  point \\((1,b)\\) is on the diagonal \\(d\\) exactly when \\(b=n\\); otherwise\n  it is on the vertical line \\(v_1\\) which is **not** present.  To avoid this\n  difficulty we modify the base family as follows: replace the missing\n  vertical \\(x=1\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (already present) and note\n  that every point with \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(1+b\\le n+1\\), i.e.\n  \\(b\\le n\\).  Such a point either lies on the diagonal \\(d\\) (when\n  \\(b=n\\)) or on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  For \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\) the line\n  \\(y=b\\) coincides with the vertical line \\(v_b\\) after a 90° rotation; in\n  our present configuration we do not have those horizontals, but we can\n  observe that the point \\((1,b)\\) also belongs to the line \\(v_{b}\\) after a\n  reflection across the line \\(x=y\\).  To keep the argument elementary we\n  simply add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) to the family and delete the horizontal\n  line \\(h\\); the resulting family still has \\(n\\) lines and the covering\n  argument becomes straightforward.  Consequently we adopt the following\n  *final* base family.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B'=\\{d,\\;x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(|\\mathcal B'|=2+(n-3)=n\\).  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\):\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and the point lies on the diagonal \\(d\\) only\n  when \\(a=n\\); otherwise it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is\n  absent from \\(\\mathcal B'\\).  To remedy this we keep the horizontal line\n  \\(y=1\\) instead of one of the other horizontals; the precise choice is\n  irrelevant because all horizontals are interchangeable.  Hence we finally\n  take  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal B''=\\{d,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\cup\\{y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}},\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhich again contains exactly \\(n\\) lines.  The covering verification is now\ntrivial:\n\n* Points with \\(a=1\\) are on \\(x=1\\);\n* Points with \\(b=1\\) are on \\(y=1\\);\n* Points with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) satisfy \\(b\\le n-2\\) (otherwise\n  \\(a+b>n+1\\) or the point lies on the diagonal \\(d\\)).  Hence such a point\n  lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that belongs to \\(\\mathcal B''\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal B''\\) indeed covers all of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Introducing sunny lines  \n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal B''\\) contains exactly three non‑sunny lines\n(\\(d,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\)) and \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines, all non‑sunny.  \nTo obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\nwe replace \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines by sunny lines, keeping the two\nmandatory non‑sunny lines \\(d\\) and \\(x=1\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{\\,y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\,\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal B''\\) (there are \\(n-3\\) of them).\nChoose any subset \\(S\\subset H\\) with \\(|S|=k\\) (possible because \\(k\\le n-2\\)).\nFor each \\(y=t\\in S\\) pick a distinct slope \\(\\alpha_t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_t\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=\\alpha_t x + (t-\\alpha_t\\cdot 1),\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the point \\((1,t)\\).  The point \\((1,t)\\) is already\ncovered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\); therefore after we delete the horizontal\nline \\(y=t\\) and insert \\(\\ell_t\\) the covering of \\((1,t)\\) is preserved.\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(y=t\\) have abscissa \\(a\\ge 2\\); such\npoints also lie on the diagonal \\(d\\) when \\(a+t=n+1\\) or on another horizontal\nline \\(y=t'\\) with \\(t'\\neq t\\).  Consequently none of them loses a covering\nwhen \\(y=t\\) is removed.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal B''\\setminus S\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_t\\mid y=t\\in S\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(\\mathcal B''\\) has \\(n\\) lines; we delete \\(|S|=k\\) lines and add the same\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines, so \\(|\\mathcal C_k|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(\\ell_t\\) (\\(t\\in S\\)) are sunny; all other lines are\nparallel to one of the forbidden directions.  Hence \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nWe have just argued that every point of \\(T_n\\) remains on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal C_k\\).  Therefore \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) satisfies the required condition.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\nall these values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 2.1 gives the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit families \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) constructed above show that every\n\\(k\\) in this range can indeed be realized.  Consequently the set of all\nnon‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
