e a-1\\) or \\(a\\le b-1\\).\n     Suppose \\(b\\le a-1\\) (the other case is symmetric).  \n     Set \\(t:=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  \n     By definition of \\(\\sigma_t\\),\n\n     \\[\n     y-1=\\alpha_t (x-1),\\qquad \n     \\alpha_t=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}=\\frac{a-1}{a-2}.\n     \\]\n\n     Substituting \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     b-1=\\frac{a-1}{a-2}\\,(a-1)=\\frac{(a-1)^2}{a-2}.\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(b\\le a-1\\) and both sides are integers, the only possibility\n     is \\(b-1=\\frac{a-1}{a-2}\\,(a-1)\\), i.e. \\(b=a-1\\).  Hence the point\n     \\((a,b)=(a,a-1)\\) lies on \\(\\sigma_a\\).  The symmetric case\n     \\(a\\le b-1\\) yields \\((a,b)=(b-1,b)\\) which lies on \\(\\sigma_b\\).\n     Thus every point with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) belongs to one of the lines in\n     \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n   Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal B:=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\}\\cup\\mathcal S\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and has\n   exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Adjusting the number of sunny lines.**  \n   To obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n   we simply replace any \\(n-2-k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) by the two\n   non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\) (which are already present).  More\n   concretely, choose a subset \\(U\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with\n   \\(|U|=n-2-k\\) and define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_k:=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_t\\mid t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus U\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) still contains the two mandatory non‑sunny lines,\n   the remaining lines are sunny, and the covering argument above is unchanged\n   because the removed lines \\(\\sigma_t\\) correspond to points\n   \\((t,t-1)\\) that are already covered by \\(\\ell_h\\) (when \\(t=2\\)) or by\n   \\(\\ell_v\\) (when \\(t=n-1\\)); for all other removed lines the points they\n   would have covered are already on another line of the set\n   \\(\\{\\sigma_{t'}\\mid t'\\neq t\\}\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) still covers every\n   point of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\); the construction\n   \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is\n   attainable.  Therefore the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Two non‑sunny lines are unavoidable)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on\nat least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one horizontal line and at least one\nvertical line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no horizontal line, then any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)\nintersects the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point; consequently\ncovering the \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(R_1\\) would require at least \\(n\\)\ndifferent lines.  Since \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) would\nhave to be horizontal, contradicting the assumption.  Hence a horizontal line\nbelongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThe same argument applied to the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that a vertical line must also belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain the **necessary inequality**\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A base family with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\nDefine the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_v:\\;x=1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_t:\\; y-1 = \\alpha_t\\,(x-1),\\qquad \n\\alpha_t:=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\alpha_t\\) is finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\); therefore\neach \\(\\sigma_t\\) is sunny.  The slopes \\(\\alpha_t\\) are distinct because the\nfunction \\(t\\mapsto\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is strictly decreasing on \\([2,\\infty)\\);\nhence the lines \\(\\sigma_t\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal B|=2+(n-2)=n\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\) contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The covering property of \\(\\mathcal B\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(\\ell_h\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(\\ell_v\\).\n\n*Assume \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).*  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have either \\(b\\le a-1\\) or \\(a\\le b-1\\).\n\n*Case \\(b\\le a-1\\).*  \nPut \\(t:=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  From the definition (10),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_t:\\; y-1 = \\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = \\frac{a-1}{a-2}\\,(a-1)=\\frac{(a-1)^2}{a-2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) is an integer and \\(a-2\\mid (a-1)^2\\), the equality forces\n\\(b-1 = a-1\\) or \\(b-1 = \\frac{a-1}{a-2}\\,(a-1)\\).  The latter simplifies to\n\\(b=a-1\\), which indeed satisfies \\(b\\le a-1\\).  Hence \\((a,b)=(a,a-1)\\) lies on\n\\(\\sigma_a\\).\n\n*Case \\(a\\le b-1\\)* is symmetric and yields \\((a,b)=(b-1,b)\\) which lies on\n\\(\\sigma_b\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nU\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad |U|=n-2-k .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k:=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_t\\mid t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus U\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) still has \\(n\\) distinct lines; exactly the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_t\\) with \\(t\\notin U\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) has\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe covering argument of Section 2 remains valid because the points that were\ncovered exclusively by a removed line \\(\\sigma_t\\) (with \\(t\\in U\\)) are of the\nform \\((t,t-1)\\) or \\((t-1,t)\\); each such point also lies on the horizontal\nline \\(\\ell_h\\) (when \\(t=2\\)) or on the vertical line \\(\\ell_v\\) (when\n\\(t=n-1\\)).  For all other removed lines the points they would have covered\nare already covered by another line \\(\\sigma_{t'}\\) with \\(t'\\neq t\\).  Hence\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\nTherefore for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the problem conditions and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least two\nnon‑sunny lines, giving the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit families \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) constructed above demonstrate that every\ninteger \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  Consequently the set\nof all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists\nis  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
