n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge \\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}=6\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nand for every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1.\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(2n-1)=\\frac{n^2+n-4n+2}{2}\n=\\frac{n^2-3n+2}{2}\n=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all \\(|T_n|\\) points if at least one of them is sunny, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nTherefore **no** configuration satisfying the covering condition can contain a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe covering condition can be fulfilled with \\(n\\) distinct lines, but any such family must consist entirely of non‑sunny lines. Consequently the only possible number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nThis holds for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)."
