stinctness of the lines** – the horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-intercepts, and the sunny lines have distinct constants \\(k+1-j\\); hence all \\(n\\) lines are different.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable, and no other values are possible.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the following family of \\(n\\) lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k),\\\\[2mm]\nS_j &: y = x + (k+1-j) \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe lines \\(L_i\\) are horizontal, hence non‑sunny.  \nEach line \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct because the constants on the right‑hand side are different.\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(L_b\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le k\\).  \nWrite \\(b=n-k+j\\) with \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nThen \\(a = (n+1-b)-a = k+1-j\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nb = a + (k+1-j),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_j\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines, and the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nNo value of \\(k\\) outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can occur.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe