\n\nObserve the following facts about the set \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n*If two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, then they also have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate and the same sum \\(x+y\\); consequently they cannot both belong to a non‑sunny line.*  \n\nIndeed, a non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three vectors \\(\\mathbf{d}_1,\\mathbf{d}_2,\\mathbf{d}_3\\).  \nIf a line is parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}_1\\) (horizontal), all its points have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate;  \nif it is parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}_2\\) (vertical), all its points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate;  \nif it is parallel to \\(\\mathbf{d}_3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), all its points have the same value of \\(x+y\\).\n\nSince any two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) differ in at least one of the three quantities \\(x\\), \\(y\\), \\(x+y\\), **no non‑sunny line can contain more than one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\)**. Consequently at most three points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) can be covered by non‑sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines among the \\(n\\) given lines.  \nAll points not covered by the (at most) three non‑sunny lines must be covered by sunny lines.  \nA sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so any two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lying on the same sunny line must have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\). Hence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\nTherefore the number of points that can be covered by the \\(s\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2s\\).  \nUsing (1) and the observation that at most three points are covered by non‑sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\n2s \\;\\ge\\; |\\mathcal{P}_n|-3\n      \\;=\\; \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-3 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-3 = 2(n-2)+\\frac{(n-4)(n-3)}{2}\\) is an integer, the inequality yields\n\n\\[\ns \\;\\ge\\; \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|\\mathcal{P}_n|-3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n        \\;=\\; n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining the lower bound\n\nWe now show that the three values \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) are all realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\; x+y=c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), hence each point lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_c\\).  \nThe direction vector of \\(L_c\\) is \\((1,-1)\\), which is *forbidden*; consequently the lines (3) are **not** sunny.  \n\nTo obtain sunny lines we perturb each line slightly: for a small \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde L_c:\\; x+y=c+\\varepsilon_c ,\n\\qquad\\text{with }0<\\varepsilon_c<\\varepsilon\\text{ and }\\varepsilon_c\\neq\\varepsilon_{c'}\\;(c\\neq c').\n\\]\n\nBecause the perturbations are arbitrarily small, each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) still lies on \\(\\widetilde L_c\\). Moreover the slope of \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is still \\(-1\\) up to an infinitesimal change, so \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) is **sunny** (its direction is no longer exactly parallel to any forbidden vector). The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) are distinct and cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) as above.  \nThese lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) except those with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nThe single remaining point is \\((1,n)\\) when \\(n\\ge 3\\); it is covered by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThus we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nUse the sunny lines \\(\\widetilde L_c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) (there are \\(n-2\\) of them).  \nThese cover all points with \\(a+b\\le n-1\\).  \nThe points with \\(a+b=n\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\) are not yet covered.  \nThey are exactly the two points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) together with the symmetric points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n\nThese four points are covered by the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=1 ,\\qquad H:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are parallel to a coordinate axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nConsequently we have a configuration consisting of \\(n-2\\) sunny lines and two non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (2) we know that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  \nThe explicit constructions of Sections 2.1–2.3 show that the three values  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2,\\qquad k=n-1,\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare all attainable. No larger value of \\(k\\) is possible because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nThus the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family of \\(n\\) lines exists is exactly \\(\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\)."
