r differences \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) cannot all be parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).  \nIf three points of \\(T_n\\) were collinear, the line would have to be either horizontal (all \\(b_i\\) equal), vertical (all \\(a_i\\) equal) or diagonal (all \\(a_i+b_i\\) equal), because the set \\(T_n\\) is a subset of the integer lattice and any line intersecting it in three lattice points must have rational slope with denominator dividing the differences of the coordinates; the only slopes that allow three points inside the triangle without leaving the triangle are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThus a line containing three points of \\(T_n\\) must be non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that \\(L\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each sunny line covers at most two points.  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\cdot n+2k=n^{2}-k(n-2).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\) points must be covered, so from (4)\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k(n-2)\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\ge\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Constructions attaining the bound  \n\nWrite \\(n=2m\\) or \\(n=2m+1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).**  \n\nDefine the \\(m\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-x+(i+2),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap T_n=\\{(i,\\,m+2-i),\\;(i+1,\\,m+1-i)\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(L_i\\) contains exactly two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe set of points covered by the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points, those with \\(a=m+2,\\dots ,2m+1\\), lie on the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=m+2,\\dots ,2m+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(m+1\\) such vertical lines, all non‑sunny.  \nThus we have \\(m\\) sunny and \\(m+1\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince \\(m=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\), the bound (5) is attained.\n\n---\n\n**Even \\(n=2m\\).**  \n\n*First family (minimum sunny lines).*  \nTake the sunny diagonals \\(L_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m-1\\) (so \\(m-1\\) sunny lines).  \nTogether with the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m+1\n\\]\n\nwe obtain \\(m-1\\) sunny and \\(m+1\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(n\\) lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHere \\(k=m-1=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n\n*Second family (one more sunny line).*  \nReplace the vertical line \\(x=m+1\\) by the sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_{m}:\\;y=-x+(m+2),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the point \\((m+1,1)\\in T_n\\).  \nNow we have \\(m\\) sunny lines and \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines, still \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=m=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final answer  \n\nCombining the necessary condition (5) with the explicit constructions, the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k=\\frac{n-1}{2},\\\\[4pt]\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\left\\{\\frac{n-2}{2},\\ \\frac{n}{2}\\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThese values are precisely the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is t