-----------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of  \n\\((1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,1)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,-1)\\) (diagonal).  \nThus a **non‑sunny** line is parallel to one of these three directions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \nThe same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=a\\) and for a diagonal line\n\\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)), where the number of admissible integer points is\n\\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (corrected)  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in primitive integer form \\(p\\,x+q\\,y=r\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and\n\\((p,q)\\neq(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\).  \nBecause the line is sunny, \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\); consequently \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\).\n\nAll lattice points on \\(L\\) are of the shape  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+qt,\\;y_0-pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer solution of the equation.\nTwo consecutive points differ by the vector \\((q,-p)\\), whose\n\\(\\ell_1\\)-norm equals \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\).  \n\nInside the triangle \\(\\{a\\ge1,b\\ge1,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) the projection of any\nsegment parallel to \\((q,-p)\\) has length at most \\(n\\) in the \\(\\ell_1\\) metric.\nHence at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/ (|p|+|q|)\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) lattice points of the\nform (8) can lie in the triangle.  Since \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\),\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n           =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 each of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines covers at most \\(n\\) points,\nand by Lemma 2 each sunny line covers at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n\\le (n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= n^{2}-k\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\), inequality (9) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\n\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2}{\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}\n=\n\\frac{n+2}{2}\n\\Bigl/\n\\Bigl(1+\\frac{n\\bmod 2}{2}\\Bigr)\n=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge k_{\\min}:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Constructions attaining the bound  \n\nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).*  \nConsider the \\(m\\) sunny diagonals  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-x+(i+2),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap T_n=\\{(i,\\,m+2-i),\\;(i+1,\\,m+1-i)\\},\n\\]\n\nso \\(L_i\\) contains exactly two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nAll points with abscissa \\(1\\le a\\le m+1\\) are covered by the family\n\\(\\{L_i\\}\\).  \nThe remaining points have \\(a\\ge m+2\\); they lie on the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=m+2,\\dots ,2m+1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(m+1\\) such vertical lines, all non‑sunny.  \nHence we have \\(m=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{