.\nTwo consecutive points differ by the vector \\((q,-p)\\); its \\(\\ell_{1}\\)-norm is\n\\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\).\n\nConsider the orthogonal projection of the triangle \\(T_n\\) onto the line\northogonal to \\((q,-p)\\), i.e. onto the direction \\((p,q)\\).  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\langle (a,b),(p,q)\\rangle = pa+qb .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\npa+qb \\ge p+q ,\\qquad\npa+qb \\le (|p|+|q|)(n+1)-\\min\\{p,q\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the set of projected values is an interval of length at most\n\\((|p|+|q|)n\\) in the integer lattice.  Since successive lattice points on the\nline differ by \\(|p|+|q|\\) in this projection, at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(|p|+|q|)n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n= n+1\n\\]\n\nprojected values can occur.  Removing the two extreme values that correspond\nto points outside \\(T_n\\) (they occur when either \\(a=0\\) or \\(b=0\\)) leaves at\nmost \\(n-1\\) interior projected values.  Because each interior value corresponds\nto exactly one lattice point of the line, we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n(The division by \\(2\\) comes from the fact that the step size in the projection\nis \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\).)  This proves the lemma. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Necessary condition on the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  By Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line covers at most\n\\(n\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each sunny line covers at most\n\\(s:=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n\\le (n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,s .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\) and solve (12) for \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\le n^{2}-kn+ks\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nkn-ks\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2}{\\,n-s\\,}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n\n\\[\nn-s=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2},   & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(n^{2}-\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Substituting these values\ninto (13) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{n}{2},   & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more sunny lines than the numbers displayed\nin (15).**  This is the necessary condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Sufficient constructions  \n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), configurations that attain the bounds\nin (15).\n\n---\n\n**Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).**  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-x+(i+2),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) the intersection with \\(T_n\\) is exactly the two points  \n\n\\[\n(i,\\;m+2-i),\\qquad (i+1,\\;m+1-i).\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(m\\) lines together cover all points whose abscissa is at most\n\\(m+1\\).  The remaining points have abscissa \\(m+2,\\dots ,2m+1\\); they are\ncovered by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=m+2,\\dots ,2m+1 .\n\\]\n\nAltogether we have \\(m=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}\\) sunny lines and \\(m+1\\) non‑sunny\nlines, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Even \\(n=2m\\).**  \n\n*Minimum number of sunny lines* (\\(k=m-1=\\dfrac{n-2}{2}\\)).  \nUse the sunny diagonals \\(L_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m-1\\) (as in (16) with\n\\(n=2m\\)) together with the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,m+1\\).  A direct\ncheck shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of these lines.\n\n*Maximum number of sunny lines* (\\(k=m=\\dfrac{n}{2}\\)).  \nStart from the previous configuration and replace the vertical line \\(x=m+1\\)\nby the sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_{m}:\\;y=-x+(m+2),\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the point \\((m+1,1)\\in T_n\\).  The family now consists\nof \\(m\\) sunny lines and \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines, still covering all points of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) both values \\(\\dfrac{n-2}{2}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{n}{2}\\) are\nrealised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nThe necessary upper bound (15) and the explicit constructions above show that\nthe only possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\dfrac{n-2}{2},\\ \\dfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese values are attainable, and no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur. ∎"
