            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  The set to be covered  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the points with \\(a+b=s\\) form a\nsegment of length \\(s-1\\); summing these lengths gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nA line is non‑sunny iff it is parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence at most \\(n\\) integer points.\nThe same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=a\\) and for a diagonal line\n\\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)), where the number of admissible integer points is\n\\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Sunny lines  \n\nWrite a sunny line in primitive integer form  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+q\\,y=r,\\qquad \\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwith direction vector \\((q,-p)\\).  \nBecause the line is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions,\n\\((p,q)\\) is not a multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|p|+|q|\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll lattice points on the line are  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+qt,\\;y_0-pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is a particular integer solution of (7).  \nTwo consecutive points differ by the vector \\((q,-p)\\); its \\(\\ell_{1}\\)-norm is  \n\n\\[\n\\| (q,-p) \\|_{1}=|p|+|q|\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsider the triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge0,\\;y\\ge0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nProject \\(\\Delta_n\\) orthogonally onto the direction \\((p,q)\\); the projection\nof a point \\((x,y)\\) is the scalar product \\(\\langle (x,y),(p,q)\\rangle =px+qy\\).\nFor \\((x,y)\\in\\Delta_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n0\\le px+qy\\le (|p|+|q|)(n+1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nHence the set of projected values of points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) is contained in the\ninteger interval  \n\n\\[\nI=[\\,\\alpha,\\;\\beta\\,],\\qquad \n\\beta-\\alpha\\le (|p|+|q|)(n+1)-2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n(The two extreme values correspond to the points \\((0,0)\\) and \\((n+1,0)\\) or\n\\((0,n+1)\\), which are **not** in \\(T_n\\) because \\(a,b\\ge1\\).)\n\nNow let a point of \\(T_n\\) lie on the line (7).  Its projection equals\n\\(px+qy=r\\).  As we move from one lattice point of the line to the next,\nthe projection changes by exactly \\(|p|+|q|\\) (by (10)).  Therefore the\ndifferent projected values that can occur for points of \\(T_n\\) form an\narithmetic progression inside the interval \\(I\\) with step \\(|p|+|q|\\).\n\nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(T_n\\) on the line is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{\\beta-\\alpha}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(|p|+|q|)(n+1)-2}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= \\Bigl\\lfloor n+\\frac{n-1}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\), the right‑hand side does not exceed\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nfor every sunny line \\(L\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  By Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line contributes at\nmost \\(n\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each sunny line contributes at most\n\\(s:=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le (n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,s .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\) and solving (14) for \\(k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}2}{\\,n-s\\,}\n=\n\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\nSince  \n\n\\[\nn-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2},   & n\\ \\text{even},\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nthe inequality simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\n\\dfrac{n}{2},   & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can contain more sunny lines than the numbers displayed\nin (15).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Constructions attaining the bound  \n\nAll constructions use **sunny lines of slope 1**, i.e. lines of the form\n\\(y=x+c\\); such lines are sunny because their direction vector \\((1,1)\\) is not\nparallel to any of the forbidden directions.\n\n---\n\n**Odd \\(n=2m+1\\).**  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=x+(i+1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,m .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap T_n=\\{(i,\\;i+1),\\;(i+1,\\;i)\\},\n\\]\n\nso each line covers exactly two points of \\(T_n\\).  These \\(2m\\) points are all\nthose with both coordinates \\(\\le m+1\\).  The remaining points have abscissa\n\\(m+2,\\dots ,2m+1\\); they are covered by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=m+2,\\dots ,2m+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe family consists of \\(m=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}\\) sunny lines and \\(m+1\\) non‑sunny\nlines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of them.\n\n---\n\n**Even \\(n=2m\\).**  \n\n*Minimum number of sunny lines* (\\(k=m-1=\\dfrac{n-2}{2}\\)).  \nUse the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m-1\\) (as in (16) with\n\\(n=2m\\)) together with the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,m+1\\).  A direct\ncheck shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of these lines.\n\n*Maximum number of sunny lines* (\\(k=m=\\dfrac{n}{2}\\)).  \nStart from the previous configuration and replace the vertical line\n\\(x=m+1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{m}:\\;y=x+(m+1),\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the point \\((m+1,1)\\in T_n\\).  The resulting family now\ncontains \\(m\\) sunny lines and \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and still covers all points\nof \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) both values \\(\\dfrac{n-2}{2}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{n}{2}\\) are\nrealised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nThe necessary upper bound (15) and the explicit families above show that the\nonly possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\dfrac{n-2}{2},\\ \\dfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThese values are attainable, and no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
