cissa \\(m+2,\\dots ,2m+1\\); they are covered by\n   the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=m+2,\\dots ,2m+1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The family consists of exactly \\(m=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}\\) sunny lines and\n   \\(m+1\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(n\\) distinct lines, and a direct inspection\n   of (5)–(6) shows that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   *Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \n\n   – **Minimum number of sunny lines** (\\(k=m-1=\\dfrac{n-2}{2}\\)).  \n     Use the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m-1\\) (as in (5) with\n     \\(n=2m\\)) together with the vertical lines  \n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,m+1 .\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n     *Verification.*  \n     For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{2m}\\) we have two cases:\n\n     1. If \\(a\\le m\\) then \\(b\\le 2m+1-a\\le m+1\\).  \n        If \\(a=b\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is in (7)).  \n        If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(|a-b|\\le m-1\\) and the point belongs to the sunny\n        line \\(L_{|a-b|}\\) because \\(b-a= \\pm(|a-b|+1)\\) gives the required\n        constant term.\n\n     2. If \\(a\\ge m+1\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in (7)) contains the point.\n\n     Hence every point of \\(T_{2m}\\) is covered.\n\n   – **Maximum number of sunny lines** (\\(k=m=\\dfrac{n}{2}\\)).  \n     Start from the previous family and replace the vertical line \\(x=m+1\\) by the\n     sunny line  \n     \\[\n     L_{m}:\\;y=x+(m+1).\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n     *Verification.*  \n     The only points that were covered exclusively by the line \\(x=m+1\\) are those\n     with abscissa \\(m+1\\).  Such a point has the form \\((m+1,b)\\) with\n     \\(1\\le b\\le m\\) (because \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\)).  For each of them we have\n     \\(b=m+1-(m+1-b)=m+1-(m+1-b)\\), i.e. \\(b=m+1-(m+1-b)\\), which is exactly the\n     equation of \\(L_{m}\\); thus \\((m+1,b)\\in L_{m}\\).  Consequently no point is\n     lost after the replacement, and the family now contains \\(m\\) sunny lines\n     and \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines, still covering all of \\(T_{2m}\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   Inequality (4) shows that no configuration can have more sunny lines than the\n   numbers displayed, while the explicit families above prove that these numbers\n   are attainable.  Therefore the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[6pt]\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\dfrac{n-2}{2},\\ \\dfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
