nny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Adding an arbitrary number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Choice of sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,1)\\in T_n,\\qquad\n   S_j:\\; y-1=(j+2)(x-j) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(j+2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\n   each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  The slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)\n   are pairwise distinct.\n\n2. **Remaining non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Keep the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=k+1,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   and the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are non‑sunny.\n\n3. **The whole family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{D\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L_k|=k+(n-1-k)+1=n .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\((a,b)\\) lies on some sunny line \\(S_j\\), we are done.*\n\n   *Otherwise \\((a,b)\\) is not on any \\(S_j\\).*\n   - If \\(a\\le k\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n     For \\(a\\le k\\) the line \\(x=a\\) coincides with the sunny line \\(S_a\\) at the\n     single lattice point \\((a,1)\\); all other points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate\n     are covered by the vertical line \\(L_a\\) which we have **replaced** by the\n     sunny line \\(S_a\\).  Since \\((a,b)\\neq (a,1)\\) (otherwise it would be on\n     \\(S_a\\)), the only remaining vertical line with that \\(x\\)-coordinate is\n     \\(L_a\\) when \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  Hence if \\(a\\le k\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq (a,1)\\) the\n     point is covered by the sunny line \\(S_a\\); the case \\((a,b)=(a,1)\\) is\n     already covered by \\(S_a\\).  Thus all points with \\(a\\le k\\) are covered.\n\n   - If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then the vertical line \\(L_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and\n     contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - The only point not lying on any vertical line is \\((n,1)\\); it satisfies\n     \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of exactly \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\)\nsunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \nLemma 2 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable\nconfiguration exists.  No restriction other than \\(k\\le n\\) can arise, because we\nalways have at most \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
