\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ the column\n   \\[\n   C_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\n   \\]\n   contains the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) (already on the diagonal) and at least one\n   further point \\((i,1)\\).  The only lines that can contain two distinct points\n   of the same column are vertical lines; a sunny line meets a column in at most\n   one point.  Consequently, to cover all points of a column we must either\n   keep the vertical line \\(x=i\\) or use a separate sunny line for each\n   non‑top point of that column.  Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, at\n   most \\(n-2\\) of them can be sunny: one line is forced to be the diagonal,\n   and at least one vertical line is required to cover the non‑top points of\n   some column.  Hence any feasible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (Lemma 3).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) in this range.  \n   *Diagonal line*  \n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n   (non‑sunny).  \n\n   *Vertical lines*  \n   Keep the vertical lines \\(x=i\\) for all \\(i\\) with \\(k+2\\le i\\le n-1\\).  \n   There are \\((n-1)-(k+2)+1=n-k-2\\) such lines; each is non‑sunny.\n\n   *Sunny lines*  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define a sunny line\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y-1=(j+2)(x-j).\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(j+2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); the slopes are distinct, so the\n   \\(S_j\\) are pairwise distinct and sunny.\n\n   *Additional vertical line*  \n   Keep the vertical line \\(x=k+1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n\n   The total number of lines is\n   \\[\n   1\\;(D)+\\underbrace{(n-k-2)}_{\\text{vertical }i\\ge k+2}\n   +1\\;(x=k+1)+k\\;(S_j)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(a\\ge k+2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to the family and\n     contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - If \\(a=k+1\\) the line \\(x=k+1\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - If \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line\n     \\(S_a\\) because \\(S_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) and has slope \\(a+2\\); the\n     equation of \\(S_a\\) can be rewritten as\n     \\[\n     b-1=(a+2)(a-a)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b=1 .\n     \\]\n     Hence \\(S_a\\) covers the *bottom* point \\((a,1)\\) of column \\(a\\).\n     The remaining points of column \\(a\\) have coordinates\n     \\((a,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  All those points satisfy\n     \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lie on the diagonal line \\(D\\) **iff**\n     \\(b=n+1-a\\); the unique top point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) is indeed on \\(D\\).\n     The points with \\(2\\le b\\le n-a\\) are covered as follows:\n     each such point belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\) **if** we keep that\n     vertical line.  In our construction we have *removed* the vertical lines\n     for \\(a\\le k\\); however, for every \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-a\\) we have\n     \\(a+b\\le n\\), so the point \\((a,b)\\) also lies on the sunny line\n     \\(S_{b-1}\\) because\n     \\[\n     b-1=(b-1)+(a-a)=(b-1)+(a+2)(a-(b-1)) ,\n     \\]\n     i.e. the line \\(S_{b-1}\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  Since \\(b-1\\le k\\)\n     (because \\(a+b\\le n\\le k+2\\) by the hypothesis \\(k\\le n-2\\)),\n     the line \\(S_{b-1}\\) is present in the family.  Thus every interior point\n     of the column is covered by a sunny line whose index is \\(b-1\\).\n\n   - The point \\((n,1)\\) is the unique point with \\(a=n\\); it lies on the\n     diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the\n   constructed family, which contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is possible.  \n   Lemma 2 proves the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Lemma 3 provides, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit\n   configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and\n   containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   He